                "years": "1955 - Present",
                "region": "experimental",
                "era": "experimental-modern",
                "quote": "I despise stories, as they mislead people into believing that something has happened. In fact, nothing happens as we flee from one condition to another.",
                "bio": "The Hungarian master of cinematic entropy. Béla Tarr creates monolithic, devastatingly slow films that document the existential decay of post-communist society, focusing on the relentless, miserable passage of time.",
                "style": "Monochrome cinematography, incredibly long takes (often 10+ minutes), relentless rain/wind, apocalyptic atmosphere, and the choreography of crowds.",
                "techniques": [
                    "The Endurance Take: Shoots scenes in single, unbroken takes that last so long the audience goes through stages of boredom, frustration, and eventually, a hypnotic state of transcendence.",
                    "Choreographing Decay: Orchestrates massive, incredibly complex camera moves through mud, rain, and decaying architecture, synchronized perfectly to melancholic accordion scores.",
                    "The Rejection of Plot: Purposely eliminates traditional plot mechanics, arguing that true cinema is found in the physical weight of human faces and the spaces they inhabit."
                ],
                "lessons": [
                    "Atmosphere is absolute. If the wind is howling and the mud is deep, the audience will feel the despair of the characters without them saying a word.",
                    "Demand patience. Do not coddle the viewer with quick cuts; force them to inhabit the physical reality of the moment."
                ],
