                "year": 2004,
                "country": "USA",
                "duration": "108 min",
                "lesson": "Non-Linear Structure & Visual Metaphor",
                "why": "Charlie Kaufman breaks structural rules by placing the climax in the middle, telling a romance in reverse, and externalizing memory deletion as physical rooms collapsing around the characters.",
                "takeaway": "Feel free to break linear time. The human brain thinks in memories and emotions, not in calendar dates. A non-linear script can represent a character's emotional truth far better than a straight timeline.",
                "scenes": [
                    {
                        "title": "The Collapsing Beach House",
                        "time": "01:22:10",
                        "description": "Joel desperately clings to his memory of Clementine as the beach house they are in physically crumbles into the ocean.",
                        "analysis": "Kaufman translates a psychological concept (forgetting) into a physical action. Instead of Joel just crying, he has to run through a house that is dissolving, illustrating the agony of losing a loved one's memory."
                    },
                    {
                        "title": "The Opening Train Meet-Cute",
                        "time": "00:04:30",
                        "description": "Joel and Clementine meet on a train to Montauk, speaking awkwardly but feeling an inexplicable draw.",
                        "analysis": "This opening sequence feels like a standard romantic comedy, but the writer is tricking us: they have actually already dated and erased each other. The dialogue has an undercurrent of deja-vu that makes sense only on repeat viewing."
                    }
                ],
                "releaseDate": "March 9, 2004",
                "writer": "Charlie Kaufman",
                "cinematographer": "Ellen Kuras",
                "editor": "Valdís Óskarsdóttir",
                "composer": "Jon Brion",
                "studio": "Anonymous Content, This is that",
                "poster": "https://m.media-amazon.com/images/M/MV5BMTY4NzcwODg3Nl5BMl5BanBnXkFtZTcwNTEwOTMyMw@@._V1_SX300.jpg",
                "plot": "Joel Barish (Jim Carrey) wakes up one Valentine's Day morning feeling inexplicably drawn to travel to Montauk. On the train, he meets Clementine (Kate Winslet), a free-spirited, blue-haired woman who speaks to him as if they already know each other. They don't — or so they think. Joel soon discovers a devastating truth: Clementine had a medical procedure performed by the Lacuna Corporation to erase all memory of him after their relationship fell apart. In grief and rage, Joel undergoes the same procedure. But as his memories of Clementine are systematically deleted — replayed in reverse, from the bitter end back to the beautiful beginning — he desperately tries to hide her inside his mind, to hold onto what he is losing. The film asks: even if love causes pain, would you erase it?"
